WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.280 --> 00:00:09.320
What is an operating system? It's a good question. We've been talking a lot about operating systems

2
00:00:09.320 --> 00:00:15.240
We never really actually defined what one is and you might not have actually given a much thought to what an operating system is

3
00:00:15.400 --> 00:00:20.400
Even though you kind of know what one is you have one on your phone your computer your game console

4
00:00:20.960 --> 00:00:27.600
An operating system or an OS is a collection of programs that manages the internal workings of the computer

5
00:00:27.760 --> 00:00:31.160
So when a program wants to write data of then retrieve it later

6
00:00:31.160 --> 00:00:37.440
The OS ensures that the application is able to do so as quickly easily and safely as possible

7
00:00:37.840 --> 00:00:45.080
An operating system has a number of management functions process management security management file management

8
00:00:45.480 --> 00:00:49.080
communications management resource management and memory management

9
00:00:49.280 --> 00:00:53.360
The way all these work together is really what makes up the heart of the OS

10
00:00:53.840 --> 00:01:01.200
Typically applications running on an operating system interface with the operating system through a simplified series of interfaces called

11
00:01:01.400 --> 00:01:02.720
API's or

12
00:01:02.720 --> 00:01:04.720
Application programming interfaces

13
00:01:05.040 --> 00:01:11.160
Let's get into one of those functions process management typically a process can be in one of five states

14
00:01:11.560 --> 00:01:19.720
New ready running waiting and terminated in the new state the process is in the stage of being created

15
00:01:19.720 --> 00:01:24.720
It's being built in the ready state the process is ready. It's waiting for pickup

16
00:01:24.720 --> 00:01:30.680
But the CPU is busy doing something else in the running state the CPU has grabbed that process

17
00:01:30.680 --> 00:01:33.240
It's picked it up and it's actively working with it

18
00:01:33.720 --> 00:01:39.560
Okay, now the waiting state. This is where it gets a little bit interesting a process typically doesn't just start and end

19
00:01:39.560 --> 00:01:44.100
All by itself it might need to read some data prompt the user for some input

20
00:01:44.100 --> 00:01:49.680
Or it might have to kick off a couple sub processes and while it's doing that while it's waiting for them to fit

21
00:01:49.680 --> 00:01:53.820
Finish the process can be put into a wait state and when it's in a wait state

22
00:01:53.820 --> 00:02:00.120
It's it's waiting and finally when the process is accomplished its task and there's nothing else for it to do

23
00:02:00.120 --> 00:02:02.120
It goes into the terminated state

24
00:02:02.720 --> 00:02:05.120
So you can see the general flow of things here

25
00:02:05.120 --> 00:02:11.160
It starts out new and then it goes into ready and then it goes into running from there

26
00:02:11.160 --> 00:02:16.160
It either goes into the terminated state which says it's done for one reason or another

27
00:02:16.160 --> 00:02:21.160
It either did everything it needed to do or something else happened or it goes back to ready

28
00:02:21.480 --> 00:02:25.240
There are two ways it can go back to ready the first is in the case of a wait

29
00:02:25.600 --> 00:02:32.160
Where it's waiting for more data user input or for another process to finish or if the CPU is like hey

30
00:02:32.160 --> 00:02:34.560
I know you're really busy and you're trying to do your stuff

31
00:02:34.560 --> 00:02:37.240
I've got a bunch of other programs try to do something too

32
00:02:37.240 --> 00:02:43.800
So I'll be right back and that's called an interrupt and interrupt is just that it kind of it interrupts the process

33
00:02:43.800 --> 00:02:51.200
And says sit right here be patient go into a ready state and I'll come back and get you and finish you when I have time to do that

34
00:02:51.720 --> 00:02:58.920
That's the only way that a computer can work on multiple programs at the same time now a process can go back and forth between

35
00:02:58.920 --> 00:03:01.880
Running and ready a whole bunch of times before it completes

36
00:03:02.240 --> 00:03:07.360
That's a good thing because if programs could only run in a straight line from ready to finished

37
00:03:07.640 --> 00:03:10.720
We wouldn't be able to run more than one program at a time on a computer

38
00:03:11.320 --> 00:03:13.320
Then there's security management

39
00:03:13.320 --> 00:03:19.880
Obviously security very very important on a mainframe because we're dealing with the world's most important financial data

40
00:03:20.080 --> 00:03:25.600
Health records personal records so security very important. We're going to talk more about that later

41
00:03:26.080 --> 00:03:33.680
Up next file management on the Z we try to offload as much of the work away from the application to the OS as possible

42
00:03:33.680 --> 00:03:38.400
So rather than making the application keep track of where everything was stored and where to get later

43
00:03:38.680 --> 00:03:40.880
We let the file management handle that

44
00:03:42.040 --> 00:03:48.520
Communications management takes care of all the network communications very very important and then there's the resource management

45
00:03:48.520 --> 00:03:52.520
This makes sure that all of the programs running on the system played nicely with one another

46
00:03:52.720 --> 00:03:55.880
It looks at everything competing for the same resources like

47
00:03:56.280 --> 00:04:02.160
CPU memory network disk and it makes sure everyone kind of gets a fair spot at those resources

48
00:04:02.800 --> 00:04:06.600
resource management is the cornerstone of modern computing and

49
00:04:07.160 --> 00:04:12.120
Lastly, there's memory management. We're going to get into this in much greater detail later on

50
00:04:12.120 --> 00:04:14.720
But suffice to say on the mainframe

51
00:04:14.720 --> 00:04:21.640
We want our applications to run as quickly and efficiently as possible and one way of doing that is by abstracting all the

52
00:04:21.840 --> 00:04:30.400
Complexities of memory management away from the developer and their applications and handling them in the memory management function and there you have it

53
00:04:30.400 --> 00:04:33.520
That's your six management functions of an operating system now

54
00:04:33.680 --> 00:04:37.840
Some of them might have seen obvious while others might not seem all that vital

55
00:04:37.920 --> 00:04:44.480
But I tell you what you take away any one of them and you'd have a really hard time getting anything done on a modern operating system
